{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "4. Names, Identity and Collisions",
  "description": "Turning a book title into a filename that survives a Kindle, without breaking the identity round trip the rerun depends on.",
  "nextTour": "5. Threads, Ctrl-C and the Log",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Two decisions per book",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "Two separate decisions are made about every exported book",
      "description": "What to call the file, and how to decide whether that book has already been exported. A policy answers both.\n\nFour exist: `PassthroughNaming` (the default, no dependencies), `StripNaming` (standard library, keeps the script), `PortableNaming` (uses `disarm`, transliterates), and `MetadataNaming` (`--name-by author-title`, wraps one of the others). Only `PortableNaming` needs an extra."
    },
    {
      "title": "An optional dependency, imported honestly",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "try:\n    import disarm",
      "description": "`disarm` is an extra. The import is guarded, the module-level name becomes `None`, and `PortableNaming.__init__` raises `PortableNamesUnavailableError` with an actionable message (`PORTABLE_HINT`).\n\n`run.main` turns that into exit code `6` before any work starts. The CI has a whole job — `test-minimal` — asserting that `disarm` never leaks into the base install."
    },
    {
      "title": "The trap: split before you clean",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "def split_extension\\(name: str\\) -> tuple\\[str, str\\]:",
      "description": "This function exists because of a real, silent, self-perpetuating bug. Read the docstring in full.\n\nCleaning the whole name and splitting afterwards lets the trailing-dot strip eat the dot separating the extension, whenever the stem cleans away to nothing. A book titled `?` gives `?.epub` → `.epub` → `epub`.\n\nThat name has no extension for the `*.epub` glob to match. So the output directory stops recognising the book as exported, and every rerun exports it again — for ever, with the whole test suite passing throughout.\n\nThe general lesson: name the component a cleanup targets, and apply it to that component alone."
    },
    {
      "title": "The strip that caused it",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "# Windows silently drops a trailing dot or space",
      "description": "The rule is right about its target and was wrong about its reach.\n\nWindows silently drops a trailing dot or space, so a name ending in one would not round-trip. Correct. But the strip now runs on the **stem** and, separately, inside the extension — never on the assembled name."
    },
    {
      "title": "What actually breaks on other filesystems",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "def _replace_illegal\\(text: str, separator: str\\) -> str:",
      "description": "Nine ASCII metacharacters and the control range. Whitespace is collapsed on the way out.\n\nNote what is **not** touched: non-Latin scripts. Every filesystem in play stores them correctly, so the portability problem is the metacharacters, the reserved device names (`CON`, `NUL`, `COM1`… in `RESERVED_STEMS`) and the length — not the script. `StripNaming` is therefore nearly loss-free, which is why a bare `--portable-names` selects it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Bytes, not characters",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "def truncate_bytes\\(text: str, limit: int\\) -> str:",
      "description": "The 255 limit on ext4, exFAT and APFS is **bytes**, and a Japanese title hits it three times sooner than an English one.\n\nThe cut tries up to `MAX_UTF8_SEQUENCE` positions, backing off a byte at a time until the prefix decodes under `surrogatepass`, so a character sliced through the middle is dropped rather than raising."
    },
    {
      "title": "Budget arithmetic",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "    budget = max_bytes - len\\(encode_name\\(extension\\)\\)",
      "description": "The extension is reserved first, then the stem gets what is left, then a trailing dot or space exposed by the cut is stripped again — falling back to `_` rather than to nothing.\n\nThe `budget < 1` branch is the degenerate case: an extension that alone exceeds the limit leaves nothing worth preserving, so the whole name is clamped and the loss accepted."
    },
    {
      "title": "The protocol",
      "file": "epubconvert/utils/policy.py",
      "pattern": "class NamingPolicy\\(Protocol\\):",
      "description": "Structural typing — a policy needs `label`, `max_bytes`, `needs_metadata`, `filename()` and `identity()`, and nothing needs to inherit anything.\n\nRead the comment on `max_bytes`. Passthrough deliberately declares **no** budget: its names come from the source directory and are already valid, and truncating one would break the round trip that rerun safety depends on."
    },
    {
      "title": "Four policies, three notions of sameness",
      "file": "epubconvert/export/naming.py",
      "pattern": "class PassthroughNaming:",
      "description": "The classes below differ mainly in how loose `identity()` is. The fourth, `MetadataNaming`, borrows the identity of the policy it wraps:\n\n- **Passthrough** — identity is the filename. Two books are the same book only if their names match exactly.\n- **Strip** — `casefold()`, so `The Hobbit.epub` and `THE HOBBIT.epub` are one book. It does not fold accents, because doing that without transliterating is not something the standard library offers.\n- **Portable** — `disarm.catalog_key`, case *and* accent insensitive, at the cost of turning `こころ.epub` into `kokoro.epub`.\n\nThe looser the identity, the more books collide — which is the next half of this tour."
    },
    {
      "title": "Collision candidates",
      "file": "epubconvert/run/claims.py",
      "pattern": "def suffixed\\(filename: str, position: int, max_bytes: int\\) -> str:",
      "description": "Position 1 is the name itself; 2 upward append ` (2)`, ` (3)` and so on, up to `MAX_SUFFIX`.\n\nThe suffix is applied **within** the policy's byte budget. Appending to a name already at the limit would push it over, and the export would fail at the closing rename with a filesystem error rather than a name collision — a confusing way to learn that two books share a title.\n\nA policy declaring no budget is left alone, for the reason given with the protocol."
    },
    {
      "title": "Deterministic assignment, and its limit",
      "file": "epubconvert/run/planning.py",
      "pattern": "def assign_names\\(",
      "description": "Names are assigned walking the packages in **sorted** order rather than selection order, so the same set of packages always produces the same names regardless of shuffling.\n\nUnder `--on-collision suffix`, every member of a crowded group is marked with a digest of its own `dc:identifier` rather than its position, so adding a book that sorts earlier no longer renames every later one.\n\nThe docstring is frank about what still moves: a book entering or leaving a collision gains or loses its marker, and a book whose identifier is junk or shared keeps the positional ` (2)` suffix, because a marker that pretended to be stable would be worse than a number that admits it is not."
    },
    {
      "title": "A losing collision is not an error",
      "file": "epubconvert/run/planning.py",
      "pattern": "claims\\.exhaust\\(group, limit\\)",
      "description": "An empty filename marks a package that lost. Under `--on-collision skip` that is anything after the first claimant; under `suffix` it takes a 100th book wanting one name, once `MAX_SUFFIX` candidates are used up by books with no identifier to tell them apart.\n\n`_decide` turns it into a `collision` decision with a reason, which is reported and counted, never written."
    },
    {
      "title": "The status vocabulary is a public contract",
      "file": "epubconvert/run/planning.py",
      "pattern": "#: What the planner can decide about a package",
      "description": "`pending`, `exported`, `collision`, `drm`, `incomplete`, `orphan`. `--list` names them all in its help text and `--list --json` emits them verbatim, so these strings are API, not internal detail.\n\nTyping them as a `Literal` keeps a mistyped comparison from passing the type checker."
    },
    {
      "title": "One table instead of three branches",
      "file": "epubconvert/run/reporting.py",
      "pattern": "#: Everything the reporter needs to know about a status, in one place",
      "description": "Each non-pending status except `orphan` maps to a counter name, a severity, a per-book line and a closing tally.\n\nThe comment records what this replaced: an if/elif chain over the statuses plus a second block of `if report.x` tallies below it. The same mapping stated twice, so a new status had to be added in three places — and two of the branches had drifted into being byte-identical.\n\nEvery entry uses the same two named fields, `name` and `reason`, so the caller never has to work out which arguments a particular message wants."
    },
    {
      "title": "Two renderings of one plan",
      "file": "epubconvert/run/reporting.py",
      "pattern": "def render_listing\\(decisions: Sequence\\[Decision\\], as_json: bool\\) -> str:",
      "description": "`--list` prints an aligned table with a count per status; `--list --json` emits the same decisions with source and target paths, `ensure_ascii=False` so titles stay readable.\n\nBoth come from `plan_exports`, plus the orphans, judged against the whole library (sharing the plan's name assignment when nothing narrows the run), which is the point: the listing cannot drift from what a run would do, because it *is* what a run would do."
    }
  ]
}
